  "worked_solution": "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one-by-one.\n\n- The function $\\frac{{t}^{2}+4t}{t}$ is continuous everywhere except the excluded point $t=0.$\n\n- The function ${t}^{2}-1$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $\\frac{1}{t-2}$ is continuous everywhere except the point $t=2.$\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous everywhere except the points $t=0$ and $t=2.$ So the interval of continuity is $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$",
